Dear Dr. Sarah Au,
On behalf of Sika’s Roofing Business Unit, we appreciate the opportunity to submit comments in 
response to the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency’s (EPA) prioritization of vinyl chloride 
(CASRN 75-01-4) under the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA). As a leader in sustainable 
roofing solutions, Sika believes in balancing environmental responsibility with the essential role 
that materials like vinyl chloride play in modern construction and building technologies. We 
support the comments filed by the Vinyl Institute (VI) and would like to expand on several key 
points that are critical for our industry and its continued economic and environmental 
contributions.
Economic Significance and Vital Applications
Vinyl chloride is a critical component in the production of polyvinyl chloride (PVC), a material 
that underpins many essential products across a wide range of industries. For Sika Roofing, 
PVC is integral to our roofing membranes which provide beneficial environmental attributes 
such as exceptional durability, improved building envelope energy efficiency, fire resistance, and 
recyclability supporting a circular economy. 
Comprehensive Regulatory Framework
Vinyl chloride and its derivatives, including PVC, are subject to extensive regulatory oversight 
that ensures safety for human health and the environment. Existing regulations under the Clean 
Air Act, OSHA standards, and RCRA, among others, have proven effective in managing any 
potential risks associated with vinyl chloride. It is essential that EPA recognizes the robustness 
of this regulatory framework, which mandates that residual vinyl chloride in PVC is reduced to 
trace amounts, thereby minimizing any health or environmental concerns. Sika Roofing and the 
broader PVC manufacturing sector have long adhered to these stringent standards, reflecting 
our commitment to safety and sustainability. 
Robust Data and Scientific Evaluation
Sika urges the EPA to take a comprehensive and science-based approach when evaluating the 
risks of vinyl chloride. The database of toxicological studies currently under review appears 
limited and does not fully encompass the breadth of available scientific research. VI has 
identified additional studies from resources like PubMed and assessments from the Agency for 
Toxic Substances and Disease Registry (ATSDR) and the Texas Commission on Environmental 
Quality (TCEQ) that warrant consideration. By incorporating these data, the EPA can ensure a 
more thorough and representative evaluation, ultimately supporting a decision based on the 
best available science.
Representative Occupational Exposure Data
Since the 1970s, occupational exposure to vinyl chloride in manufacturing environments has 
been significantly reduced through stringent regulations and industry safety initiatives. The EPA 
must ensure that any risk evaluation of worker exposure reflects current conditions and uses 
representative, modern data. Studies from earlier decades may not accurately reflect the 
reduced exposure levels achieved through ongoing stewardship efforts. Sika Roofing, along 

with other industry leaders, remains committed to maintaining and improving worker safety, but 
it is critical that regulatory decisions are based on relevant, up-to-date data.
De Minimis Concentrations in Downstream Products
The presence of vinyl chloride as a trace impurity in downstream products, such as PVC 
membranes, does not pose an unreasonable risk to health or the environment. Years of 
regulatory oversight under TSCA and other frameworks have confirmed that de minimis levels 
are not associated with meaningful risk. We encourage the EPA to focus its risk evaluation 
efforts on scenarios where vinyl chloride concentrations are substantial enough to warrant 
concern, and to streamline the assessment process for trace-level exposures that do not 
contribute to significant health or environmental risks.
Economic Impact
Sika Roofing, with 300+ employees, produces approximately 200 million square feet of 
membrane annually. In addition, Sika maintains a vast network of partners and business 
operations spanning private, public, academic, and government sectors, with a customer base 
that covers all North America. This network relies heavily on the production of vinyl chloride and 
roofing membranes. Any disruption in the availability of vinyl chloride could have significant 
economic consequences, affecting employment, innovation, and advancements in building 
practices across these sectors.
Conclusion
As EPA moves forward in its evaluation of vinyl chloride, we urge the agency to prioritize the use 
of the most comprehensive and up-to-date scientific data, recognize the strength of existing 
regulations, and consider the minimal risks associated with trace levels of vinyl chloride in 
downstream products. For industries like ours, vinyl chloride is not only essential but also well-
managed under the current regulatory framework. Any regulatory actions should consider the 
economic impact on industries that rely on vinyl chloride to deliver sustainable, innovative 
solutions that benefit both society and the environment.
Thank you for your consideration.
-Sika Roofing USA"
