Dear Assistant Administrator Freedhoff: 
 
The Center for Environmental Health (CEH) submits these comments to the Environmental 
Protection Agency (EPA) in connection with the proposed high-priority substance designation for 
vinyl chloride under section 6(b) of the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA).  
CEH is a national nonprofit organization whose mission is to protect public health, support 
decision-makers, and reduce exposure to toxic chemicals in our air, water, soil, food, and the 
products we use every day.  We have developed expertise in safer non-toxic built environment 
products, with a special focus on flooring and furniture.  It is this expertise, developed through more 
than 2 decades of research and advocacy, that we leverage to inform the EPA about the 
unreasonable risk to public health and the environment that vinyl chloride poses, including to 
potentially exposed and susceptible subpopulations. 
The various hazards of vinyl chloride are well documented.  Adverse health effects from acute 
exposures to vinyl chloride include central nervous system (CNS) effects, such as dizziness, 
drowsiness, and headaches in humans, while chronic exposures have resulted in liver damage and 
long-term CNS effects.1,2  Additionally, vinyl chloride is a known human carcinogen, being classified 

as a known human carcinogen by the National Institutes of Health (NIH)3, the EPA4, and the 
International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC).5   
Furthermore, there are numerous studies that point towards the dangers of vinyl chloride 
manufacture, transportation, and disposal.  During its transportation phase, at any given time there 
are up to 36 million pounds of vinyl chloride traveling through the US6, making disasters like the 
East Palestine train derailment possible at any time.  Additionally, millions of pounds of chlorinated 
wastes are burnt in the US exposing fenceline communities to harmful pollutants in the process. 
For instance, more than 20 million pounds of chlorinated wastes from vinyl chloride and polyvinyl 
chloride (PVC) plastic plants were sent to burn in incinerators in Arkansas, Louisiana, and Texas in 
2021. 6  
CEH has focused more and can comment more extensively on the environmental and public health 
impacts of the production of vinyl chloride and its use in the production of PVC flooring.  Vinyl 
chloride production in the US releases around 500,000 pounds of this chemical per year and there 
have been 966 unplanned releases, some of these extremely dangerous, reported since 2010. 7 
A report authored by CEH, Autocase Economic Advisory, and Material Research L3C titled 
Flooring’s Dirty Climate Secret9 raised several alarming points about vinyl chloride and PVC 
production.  The report concluded that carbon dioxide emissions from PVC flooring production 
were underestimated by between 8 and 180%.  Although the largest offenders were PVC flooring 
plants overseas, factories that manufacture vinyl tile flooring with PVC resins in the U.S. on average 
underestimated their carbon emissions by 27%.  This added carbon emissions exacerbate the 
problem of climate change that in turn has many adverse effects on the U.S.8 
The report also pointed out an alarming fact about vinyl chloride manufacturers.  Vinyl chloride 
factories use significant quantities of highly toxic chemicals like PFAS, asbestos, and mercury in 
their production of this chemical.  The US alone imports an estimated 373 metric tons of asbestos 
each year, most of which goes to service the chlor-alkali industry to produce chlorine.  According to 
the Asbestos Disease Awareness Organization, only 6 percent of US-produced chlorine is used to 
purify water, while 47 percent is used to produce ethylene dichloride, which is used primarily for the 
production of vinyl chloride monomer (VCM) which is primarily used for the production of PVC.1  
These chemicals are hazardous to workers all along the supply chain and endanger frontline and 
fenceline communities in the U.S. and abroad. 
All the adverse health and environmental impacts of vinyl chloride and PVC production have 
prompted certain industries and product certifying bodies to start shying away from its use.  The 
Business and Institutional Furniture Manufacturers Association (BIFMA), for example, has instituted 
a toxics-based credit (7.4.4) in its LEVEL certification that prevents the presence of PVC in furniture 
that meet it.10 Similarly, reputable 3rd party certifications such as GreenScreen Certified11, 
Greenhealth Approved12, and Cradle to Cradle13 that cover furniture and flooring products have all 
established a no PVC requirement in order to meet their certification. 
The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services has classified vinyl chloride as a known human 
carcinogen.  Inhaling vinyl chloride can increase the risk of liver cancer and can cause other health 
 
1  2023-Reinstein-Asbestos-Report-New.pdf (asbestosdiseaseawareness.org) 

problems.  Its transportation through rail places millions of people in danger of spills and 
explosions, its disposal in incinerators releases extremely harmful chemicals into the air, and its 
production releases more greenhouse gas emissions than previously estimated and requires the 
use of toxic chemicals in its manufacturing process.  Because vinyl chloride presents significant 
hazards and potential routes of exposure it clearly presents an unreasonable risk to health and the 
environment, including to potentially exposed and susceptible subpopulations.  It is for all these 
reasons that CEH urges EPA to designate vinyl chloride as a “High Priority Substance” under the 
TSCA chemical prioritization process and begin its risk evaluation process immediately.  
 
Respectfully submitted, 
Thomas R. Fox 
Senior Legislative Counsel 
 
Center for Environmental Health 
tom@ceh.org 
 
 
 

