National Ground Water Association
Response to
Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)
Proposed High-Priority Substance Designations Under the Toxic Substances 
Control Act (TSCA); Notice of Availability
Published: July 25, 2024
Comments Due: October 23, 2024
Document: 2024-16394 (89 FR 60420)
Docket Numbers: EPA-HQ-OPPT- 2023-0601; FRL-11581-03-OCSPP
Summary:
Under the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA), EPA proposed 5 chemicals for high-
priority substance designation for risk evaluation which include:
1. acetaldehyde (CASRN 75-07-0)
2. acrylonitrile (CASRN 107-13-1)
3. benzenamine (CASRN 62-53-3)
4. vinyl chloride (CASRN 75-01-4)
5. 4,4-methylene bis(2-chloroaniline) (MBOCA) (CASRN 101-14-4)
Risk evaluation could lead to restrictions on manufacturing, use or disposal of the 
chemicals to eliminate unreasonable risk to human health or the environment, OR to 
low-risk designation with no limitations.
Electronic Link: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2024/07/25/2024-
16394/proposed-high-priority-substance-designations-under-the-toxic-substances-
control-act-tsca-notice-of
National Ground Water Association Comments
While all five of the high priority chemicals could have negative impacts on groundwater
and its users if released directly to the environment, three chemicals – acrylonitrile, vinyl 
chloride and 4,4-methylene bis(2-chloroaniline) (also referred to as ‘bisamine’) – have 
the most direct relation to groundwater in their product use.
Poly Acrylonitrile (PAN) is used in the manufacture of fibers for ultra filtration 
membranes and hollow fibers for reverse osmosis (RO) applications. RO is used for 
water treatment in both remediation and water supply to remove contaminants from 
water.
Vinyl Chloride is used as a principal ingredient in the manufacture of polyvinyl chloride 
(PVC) pipe. PVC pipe is used extensively in groundwater production as well casing and 

screen and delivery of groundwater through water pipe lines. Approximately two million 
miles of PVC pipe have been installed for utility water and wastewater lines in North 
America1, not including indoor PVC water supply and wastewater plumbing in homes 
and commercial structures.
4,4-methylene bis(2-chloroaniline) is used as a curing agent for polyurethane that has 
been used for environmentally friendly grouts used to contain groundwater 
contamination or preferentially control groundwater migration in sensitive areas.
The release of any of these monomer chemicals into the environment would result in 
significant adverse health effects including cancers. However, used as monomers in the 
presence of other additive chemicals to make polymers that become products, in these 
cases applied to groundwater as filter media, pipe or pump, they become components 
of inert materials, reducing the toxicity of the original constituents. Use in the products 
has reduced their risks. The release of these chemicals from products in use would 
require breaking the bonds of the polymers through processes such as burning or 
applying acids to the products.
Certainly, the monomer forms of these chemicals present significant health risks, and 
precautions should be taken to minimize or eliminate exposure in their manufacture.
However, it should be recognized that polymers containing these monomer units will 
have different physical and chemical properties, and as such any regulations or 
guidance resulting from this action should be specifically applicable to the chemicals in 
their unbound state and not to any polymeric material containing these chemicals as 
monomer units. For example, designation of the monomer vinyl chloride as a High-
Priority Substance should not have any regulatory effects on final products, such as 
polymeric PVC pipe. Steps to preclude exposure resulting from breaking the bonds of 
the polymer forms of these chemicals should be specified to protect public health.
Thank you for the opportunity to comment on these proposed High-Priority Substance 
Designations NGWA urges the EPA to use the best available science when considering 
these chemicals for designation as High-Priority Substances.
To address questions or further information, please contact:
Charles Job, Regulatory Affairs Manager
National Ground Water Association
(202) 660-0060, cjob@ngwa.org
                                           
