EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448-0608,EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448,"Docket Number EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448 
Dear Dr. Sarah Au, 
In response to the request for public comments by the United States (U.S.) Environmental Protection 
Agency (EPA or Agency) regarding its proposed designation of vinyl chloride (CASRN 75-01-4) as a 
high priority substance under the Toxic Substances Control Act, we wish to express our support for 
comments developed by the Vinyl Institute (VI), summarized below, as well as comments filed by VI 
in the previous comment period.1 Vinyl chloride is an essential commodity chemical that is a vital 
part of the value chain for many products.  
The Plastic Pipe and Fittings Association (PPFA) member companies manufacture safe, durable and 
affordable pipes and fittings for the North American market.  These products include rigid piping 
systems made with Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC)  resins, both derived from VCM, which represent about 
half of the resin market.  These piping system products deliver potable water from municipalities to 
homes and business, distribute hot- and cold-water inside of buildings, and even take wastewater 
safely from homes and businesses to water recycling and waste treatment.  They are used in other 
applications, such as fire sprinkler systems, rainwater harvesting, industrial plants and electrical 
conduit. Piping systems made with these products do not rust or corrode. To attempt to replace these 
VCM derived rigid piping products with other materials would require significant quantities of higher 
cost and energy intensive materials such as steel, brass, copper and other metals, and repeated 
replacement due to corrosion.  Copper is one material now considered by several countries as a 
strategic material, as it’s essential and impossible to substitute for electrification, electric vehicles, 
and increasing renewable energy production for reducing carbon emissions.  
PVC piping products must meet ANSI/NSF Standard 61 to be sold in North America.  Standard 61, 
adopted by state and local health authorities across the country, effectively requires all piping and 
plumbing materials conveying potable water to meet stringent health effects criteria based on the 
latest risk assessments.  The Standard, which was developed with participation by EPA in the 1980’s, 
helps ensure safe drinking water systems, including the safe use of PVC that comes into contact with 
drinking water.  
As EPA moves through this phase of its years-long TSCA section 6 process, we want to emphasize 
several key points: 
 
1 Docket Document No. EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448-0025, available at:  
https://www.regulations.gov/search/comment?filter=EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448-0025.   

 
 
• 
Vinyl chloride is well-managed under U.S. federal law through the Clean Air Act NESHAPS 
rules, OSHA vinyl chloride standard, OSHA process safety management, and the Resource 
Conservation and Recovery Act (RCRA), in addition to state regulations. Additionally, 
polyvinyl chloride (PVC) manufacturers are required under existing regulations to remove 
residual vinyl chloride from PVC resin to minute levels that are protective of human health 
and the environment.  
• 
Vinyl chloride is already deeply researched. However, the database of hazard studies EPA 
appears ready to rely on for final prioritization and possible risk evaluation is unnecessarily 
limited, and should include additional studies identified by VI for EPA to consider. EPA 
identified the studies under consideration for use in prioritization and the possible future risk 
evaluation of vinyl chloride through their Health Assessment Workspace Collaborative 
(HAWC). VI reviewed the HAWC database to ensure the methods for identification of studies 
were comprehensive and no important hazard studies were missed in EPA’s review. VI 
identified an initial list of scientific studies relevant to human health hazard from previous 
assessments by the Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry (ATSDR), Texas 
Commission on Environmental Quality (TCEQ), and the EPA Integrated Risk Information 
System (IRIS), as well as studies available through the National Center for Biotechnology 
Information PubMed resource that were not captured in the HAWC assessment.  
• 
When assessing the potential risks to workers of vinyl chloride, EPA should consider key 
studies identified by industry on vinyl chloride. The Agency should factor in the role of 
experimental design and relevance to current work practices, because workplace exposures 
since 1975 have been dramatically decreased under subsequent regulations and 
stewardship efforts. Occupational exposure studies must be suitable for characterizing 
current exposures to vinyl chloride during manufacturing.  A VI review of EPA’s HAWC 
database found that exposure data and studies for vinyl chloride manufacturers are limited 
in quality and availability. In addition, EPA must consider the purpose of sampling when 
evaluating the relevance of a given study for characterizing worker exposure to vinyl chloride. 
Exposure studies with specific objectives may not provide a representative estimate of 
exposure during normal operating conditions. 
• 
Years of experience under many regulatory programs, including TSCA, indicate that de 
minimis concentrations and exposures do not meaningfully contribute to risk. EPA should 
consider whether it is a necessary or sensible use of agency and stakeholder resources to 
include de minimis concentrations and exposures in the scope of the risk evaluation for vinyl 
chloride, or whether de minimis concentrations and exposures could be screened early in 
the process to determine that they do not contribute to risk.  
Ahead of developing a high-quality risk evaluation for vinyl chloride using the best available 
science and a weight-of-evidence approach, as required by TSCA, we urge EPA to strongly 
consider the relevance of a more robust data set on hazards and exposures, and the positive 
impact of decades of progress in industry work practices to effectively protect worker safety and 
human health."
EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448-0610,EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448,"October 23, 2024 
 
Sarah Au 
U.S. Environmental Protection Agency 
Office of Pollution Prevention and Toxics 
Data Gathering, Management, and Policy Division (7406M) 
1200 Pennsylvania Avenue NW 
Washington, D.C. 20460 
 
Submitted electronically via: https://www.regulations.gov 
 
RE: U.S. Environmental Protection Agency Proposed High-Priority Substance Designations Under the 
Toxic Substances Control Act—Docket ID No. EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448 
 
Ms. Au: 
 
The Association of California Water Agencies (ACWA) appreciates the opportunity to provide comments 
on the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency’s (EPA) proposal to designate vinyl chloride (CASRN 75-
01-4) as a High-Priority Substance for risk evaluation under the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA). 
ACWA shares EPA’s goal to protect public health while ensuring a safe and reliable water supply. When 
deciding whether to designate vinyl chloride as a High-Priority Substance, EPA should consider the 
potential impacts on states and public water agencies including compliance efforts, infrastructure upgrades, 
and associated costs. 
 
ACWA is the largest statewide coalition of public water agencies in the country. Our more than 470 public 
agency members collectively are responsible for 90 percent of the water delivered to communities, farms, 
and businesses in California. For more than a century ACWA and our members have been working to 
provide high quality, reliable water supplies in an environmentally sustainable and fiscally responsible 
manner. 
 
EPA is an important federal partner for ACWA’s members and other water providers to address water 
challenges. ACWA agency members help EPA implement effective and sustainable initiatives for water 
supply, quality, infrastructure, and more by executing regulatory compliance, discussing technical 
assistance and best-practices, and utilizing funding opportunities. 
 
ACWA’s comments provide information and considerations concerning EPA’s decision on whether to 
designate vinyl chloride as a High-Priority Substance. 
 
I. 
EPA Should Carefully Consider Any Decision to Designate Vinyl Chloride as a High-
Priority Substance Within the Context of Water Supply Needs 
 
ACWA appreciates EPA’s commitment to public health and environmental protection by evaluating 
whether to designate vinyl chloride as a High-Priority Substance. Vinyl chloride is used in the production 
of polyvinyl chloride (PVC) pipes that are often used for water supply.1  Many ACWA member agencies, 
as well as water suppliers throughout the nation, incorporate PVC pipes as a component of the infrastructure 
used to provide water supply. 
 
 
1 California Office of Environmental Health Hazard Assessment: Vinyl chloride. https://oehha.ca.gov/chemicals/vinyl-chloride.  

Page 2 
 
 
ACWA member agencies prioritize the delivery of safe and reliable water. ACWA member agencies work 
to ensure that drinking water as well as water impacting land and aquatic ecosystems, watersheds, forests, 
recreational uses, energy production, and more are free from harmful contaminants. Through regular and 
thorough testing and investments in water treatment technologies, ACWA member agencies ensure that 
drinking water supplies are safe and comply with all state and federal drinking water regulations. 
 
ACWA understands that EPA preliminarily found vinyl chloride may present an unreasonable risk of injury 
due to hazard and exposure potential, persistence and bioaccumulation, potentially exposed or susceptible 
subpopulations, storage near significant sources of drinking water, conditions of use, and production 
volume.2 ACWA members are dedicated to providing a safe and reliable supply of water, and currently 
adhere to California’s more stringent standard for vinyl chloride an MCL of 0.05 ppb compared to EPA’s 
2 ppb. ACWA member agencies utilize PVC piping since it has been recognized as one of the safer 
alternatives for water supply.3 To ensure ACWA continues to provide a safe and reliable water supply, 
ACWA asks EPA to carefully consider the designation of vinyl chloride as a High-Priority Substance within 
the context of the need to supply water. If EPA designates vinyl chloride as a High-Priority Substance it 
would initiate the TSCA risk evaluation process to evaluate and mitigate potential unreasonable risks to 
human health and the environment from vinyl chloride using the best available science. 
 
II. 
Vinyl Chloride Designation as a High-Priority Substance Could Impact States and Public 
Water Agencies 
 
As previously stated, vinyl chloride is used in the production of PVC pipes that are often used for water 
supply. As such, many states and public water agencies could be impacted if EPA designates vinyl chloride 
as a High-Priority Substance. EPA should consider the following when deciding whether to designate vinyl 
chloride as a High-Priority Substance. 
 
Compliance: If designated as a High-Priority Substance, states—like California—and public water 
agencies—like ACWA member agencies—may need to align TSCA compliance standards for vinyl 
chloride. We ask EPA to provide clear compliance guidance for water agencies. Understanding the “rules 
of the road” will ensure water managers around the nation are able to construct water infrastructure that 
lasts for generations. We also ask that EPA provide adequate time for water agencies to comply with any 
new standards established by EPA. 
 
Infrastructure: Long-term infrastructure impacts following a final designation of vinyl chloride as a High-
Priority Substance may exist. Although California standards for vinyl chloride are stricter than EPA’s as 
previously mentioned, ACWA member agencies with older infrastructure systems may need to assess and 
potentially replace components in the future. While PVC is commonly used in water systems and 
considered a safer option than lead piping, the federal government, including EPA, may later determine that 
PVC cannot be used in water systems or place a ban on it generally, like actions it has taken with respect 
to lead pipe. A similar designation would impact water systems nationwide. While a designation of vinyl 
chloride as a High-Priority Substance would not necessarily ban PVC, ACWA is highlighting this as a 
potential long-term implication for EPA to consider. 
 
Costs: EPA does not consider costs when designating a High-Priority Substance. However, if ACWA 
member agencies need to adhere to compliance measures after a designation of vinyl chloride as a High-
Priority Substance, significant costs may be incurred. For example, ACWA member agencies could face 
costs to upgrade water treatment facilities, invest in advanced filtration systems, conduct more frequent 
water quality testing, or replace PVC piping. These costs would impact operational budgets and potentially 
 
2 U.S. Environmental Protection Agency: Proposed Designation of Vinyl Chloride as a High-Priority Substance for Risk Evaluation. 
EPA-740-P-24-002. July 2024. https://www.regulations.gov/document/EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448-0593. 
3 U.S. Environmental Protection Agency: Wastewater Technology Fact Sheet: Pipe Construction and Materials. EPA 832-F-00-
068. September 2000. https://www3.epa.gov/npdes/pubs/pipe_construction.pdf.  

Page 3 
 
 
lead to higher water rates for consumers. ACWA therefore urges EPA to consider ways to offset costs to 
public water agencies if water infrastructure upgrades are needed to mitigate vinyl chloride use and 
exposure. Additional federal funding would help offset increased infrastructure replacement costs. 
 
III. 
EPA Should Issue Guidance on Safe Water Supply Piping Options if Vinyl Chloride is 
Designated as a High-Priority Substance 
 
An estimated 2.2 million miles of pipe comprise the transmission lines and distribution mains that provide 
treated water to those served by the nation’s approximately 50,000 community water systems.4 Yet EPA 
has not issued clear guidance on which piping material(s) is the safest alternative for water supply and 
quality. ACWA recommends EPA create guidance, using the best available science, on the safest piping 
materials used for water infrastructure projects to ensure that public water agencies like ACWA member 
agencies provide safe and reliable drinking water to communities. 
 
IV. 
Conclusion 
 
ACWA thanks EPA for the opportunity to provide comments concerning its proposal to designate vinyl 
chloride as a High-Priority Substance for risk evaluation under TSCA. ACWA looks forward to 
collaborating with EPA on this important effort. Please let us know if we can provide additional information 
or be of assistance. If you have any questions or would like to follow up on this matter, please contact 
ACWA Director of Federal Relations, Ian Lyle, at ianl@acwa.com or (202) 434-4765. 
 
Respectfully, 
 
 
Ian Lyle 
Director of Federal Relations 
Association of California Water Agencies 
 
4 Congressional Research Service: Drinking Water Infrastructure Needs: Background and Issues for Congress. December 18, 2023. 
https://crsreports.congress.gov/product/pdf/R/R47878."
EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448-0596,EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448,"Michael S. Regan, Administrator
U.S Environmental Protection Agency
1200 Pennsylvania Avenue NW
Washington, D.C. 20004
RE: Docket number EPA-HQ-OPPT-2023-0601
October 21, 2024
Dear Administrator Regan,
PIRG, Public Interest Research Group, is an advocate for the health and well-being of the
public. On behalf of PIRG, Environment America, and Environmental Action Group, we are
submitting the 20,302 attached comments in support of designating the known-carcinogen vinyl
chloride as a ‘high priority’ substance under the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) and
phasing out the production, use, and sale of vinyl chloride as soon as possible. We ask that
these names be entered into the official record and considered as separate public comments.
Over 10 billion pounds of vinyl chloride are produced on average annually in the U.S., primarily
to make PVC plastic. There have been frequent and severe incidents (leaks, spills, explosions,
fires) across the United States involving vinyl chloride and given the frequency of incidents, on
average every 5.3 days, future incidents are inevitable.
Vinyl chloride is known to be harmful to public health and the environment, and with safer
alternatives available on the market, the continued use of vinyl chloride is not only not
necessary, but dangerous. Exposure to vinyl chloride can cause an increased risk for liver
cancer, brain and lung cancers, lymphoma and leukemia. Vinyl chloride is pervasive in our air,
water, and soil.
We applaud the EPA’s proposal to designate vinyl chloride as a high-priority substance. The
EPA should continue to follow the science and conclude that vinyl chloride is too dangerous for
the health and well being of people and the environment.
Please act quickly to phase out toxic vinyl chloride to ensure a safe and healthy future.
Thank you for the opportunity to provide public comments.
Sincerely,
Kelly Leviker
Beyond Plastics Advocate
PIRG

Michael S. Regan, Administrator
U.S Environmental Protection Agency
1200 Pennsylvania Avenue NW
Washington, D.C. 20004
RE: Docket number EPA-HQ-OPPT-2023-0601
U.S. Environmental Protection Agency Administrator Michael Regan,
Vinyl chloride was identified as a carcinogen 50 years ago. The 2023 train derailment, spill and
combustion of vinyl chloride in East Palestine, Ohio, was just the latest example of how
dangerous this chemical can be, and why it needs to be banned from use in plastic production
nationwide.
Let’s prevent more tragic accidents. Protect our communities and ban vinyl chloride.
Sincerely,
Adrian Cardwell, Knutsford, AA 0
Diana Tiessen, Brighton, AA 99999
Liana Alecu, Pitesti, AE 11037
