Dear Administrator Regan:
Thank you for this opportunity to comment on the U.S. Environmental Protection
Agency’s (“EPA” or the “Agency”) proposed high-priority substance designations under the
Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) (EPA-HQ-OPPT-2023-0601).
On behalf of Waterkeeper Alliance, the undersigned 12 U.S. Waterkeeper groups, and our
respective individual members and supporters, we write to affirm the importance of EPA’s
progress in finding that acetaldehyde (CASRN 75-07-0), acrylonitrile (CASRN 107-13-1),
benzenamine (CASRN 62-53-3), vinyl chloride (CASRN 75-01-4), and 4,4'-methylene
bis(2-chloroaniline) (MBOCA) (CASRN 101-14-4) are chemicals that present a significant and
unreasonable risk to human health and the environment. We urge EPA to reach this finding with
urgency to prevent this potential harm.
Waterkeeper Alliance is a global movement uniting more than 300 community-based
Waterkeeper groups around the world, focusing citizen action on issues that affect our
waterways, from pollution to climate change. The Waterkeeper movement patrols and protects
nearly six million square miles of rivers, lakes, and coastlines in the Americas, Europe,
Australia, Asia, and Africa. In the U.S., Waterkeeper Alliance represents the interests of more
than 150 U.S. Waterkeeper groups and their more than one million members and supporters that
live, work, and recreate in or near waterways across the country, many severely impaired by

pollution. In the past three years alone, Waterkeeper Alliance, Waterkeeper groups, and our
respective supporters in the U.S. have submitted more than 50,000 public comments on EPA
actions. Additionally, Waterkeeper Alliance and Waterkeeper groups regularly attend public
meetings and hearings with EPA, demonstrating our collective knowledge about the Agency’s
processes and our strong interest in engaging on issues that impact our communities, water, and
the environment.
As EPA acknowledges, this is only one step in designating the chemicals as High-Priority
Substances.
Under the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) EPA conducts risk evaluations for
chemicals to assess their danger to humans and the environment.1 If after undergoing the risk
evaluation, EPA finds a chemical to be an unreasonable risk to health or the environment, it
begins a risk management process to reduce or eliminate the risks created by the chemical.2
TSCA requires EPA to consistently designate 20 chemicals for high-priority risk evaluation, and
20 for low-priority risk evaluation.3 This proposed rule would include acetaldehyde,
acrylonitrile, benzenamine, and 4,4'-methylene bis(2-chloroaniline) as chemicals considered
high-priority for beginning their risk evaluations. This designation is critical, as chemicals
considered high-priority immediately begin their risk evaluations.4
Waterkeeper Alliance supports EPA's initial actions to list acetaldehyde, acrylonitrile,
benzenamine, vinyl chloride, and 4,4'-methylene bis(2-chloroaniline) as high-priority substances.
Given the extensive scope of plastic pollution in our water, we urge the Agency to prioritize its
resources and attention to accelerating these efforts.
Each of the Five Chemicals Listed for High Priority Substance Designation is a Threat to
Human Health and the Environment
Acetaldehyde, acrylonitrile, benzenamine, vinyl chloride, and 4,4'-methylene
bis(2-chloroaniline) are compounds used in plastic production. For example, Acetaldehyde is a
