https://www.epa.gov/assessing-and-managing-chemicals-under-tsca/prioritization-existing-chemicals-under-tsca

building block of plastics, as it is a chemical used to make other chemicals, adhesives, or
petrochemicals that are then used for plastic.5 Similarly, vinyl chloride is primarily used to make
PVC pipes and other plastics.6 Acetaldehyde, acrylonitrile, benzenamine, and 4,4'-methylene
bis(2-chloroaniline) are each currently regulated by EPA as probable human carcinogens, and
vinyl chloride has been regulated as a known human carcinogen for almost 30 years.7 The
science and experience of frontline communities demonstrate that it is past time that EPA
recognizes these chemicals as unreasonable risks to human health and the environment.8 This is
especially true for fenceline communities burdened by the impacts of these chemicals during
production and end-of-cycle disposal.
Plastics impact the environment at every part of their life cycle, from production to use to
disposal.9 An estimated 140 million tons of plastic accumulated in aquatic ecosystems by 2019.10
The spread of plastic is only going to intensify, as the Organisation for Economic Co-operation
and Development estimates that “accumulated stocks of plastics in nature would more than triple
in 2060 to an estimated amount of 493 million tons, including the marine environment (145
million tons, 5-fold increase) and freshwater ecosystems (348 million tons, 3-fold increase).""11
As a result of current plastic pollution, microplastics are found in nearly every human organ
system.12 Beyond microplastics themselves entering our bodies and our ecosystems, studies show
that it is common for more than 2000 chemicals to leach into water from a single plastic
product.13 Plastics threaten the health of every living thing on the planet, and we urgently need a
deeper understanding and stronger regulations.
