As EPA expects to complete five risk evaluations per year for the next several years, it is
essential to prioritize these five chemicals.14 Currently, more than 16,000 chemicals are used or
present in plastic materials and products, we know more than 4,200 of those to be persistent,
bioaccumulative, mobile, and/or toxic, and 1,300 chemicals of concern are used in plastics.15
Despite the risk, only 6% of the 16,000 chemicals are subject to international regulation.16
Simply put, prioritizing these five chemicals is the bare minimum EPA should be doing to
address the harmful impacts of plastic. This situation is untenable if we are to meaningfully
protect our health and environment, and the United States needs to lead in regulating these
harmful plastics.
Waterkeeper groups nationwide urge EPA to acknowledge plastic pollution as the
emergency it is and act accordingly. The science of the risk of these chemicals is clear. We
urgently need comprehensive and transparent regulation of the chemicals permeating the world
through plastic.
Respectfully submitted,
