Re: Vinyl Institute Comments on Proposed High-Priority Substance Designations under the Toxic 
Substances Control Act (TSCA) – Vinyl Chloride 
 
Dear Ms. Au:  
 
The Vinyl Institute 1 and its Vinyl Chloride TSCA Consortium2 (collectively, “VI”) appreciate the opportunity 
to provide comments on the U.S. EPA’s (EPA) proposal to designate vinyl chloride (CASRN 75-01-4) as a 
high priority substance under section 6 of the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA) based on hazard and 
potential for exposure.3 The industry is committed to the safety of our workers, customers and neighbors. 
VI provides the following comments to assist EPA with final prioritization and to provide key information 
for consideration during final prioritization and upon initiation of the risk evaluation of vinyl chloride.   
Vinyl chloride is an essential commodity chemical produced in heavily regulated facilities in the United 
States, providing great benefits that apply to a broad spectrum of products. Vinyl chloride is a data-rich 
substance that has been produced safely in the United States under federally enforceable regulations 
since the mid-1970s. Decades of investment in new process design, equipment reliability, engineering 
controls, ongoing compliance with OSHA regulations, and other safety measures to protect workers and 
the communities we serve have led to significant emissions reductions. Federal regulations significantly 
limit the potential presence of residual vinyl chloride in PVC resin. We have been willing, collaborative 
good-faith partners to the Agency over the years, and we remain committed to engaging with EPA 
throughout the TSCA regulatory process for vinyl chloride.  
 
