In the proposed designation document for vinyl chloride, EPA identified the studies under consideration 
for use in prioritization and the future risk evaluation through their Health Assessment Workspace 
Collaborative (HAWC), “an interactive, expert-driven, content management system for human health 
assessments that is intended to promote transparency, data usability, and understanding of the data and 
decisions supporting an environmental and human health assessment.” In this database, EPA identified 
studies on both hazard and exposure of vinyl chloride.   
The comments prepared herein address several items VI would like to raise to EPA’s attention during the 
final prioritization process and in anticipation of future risk evaluation. As discussed below, VI 
recommends that EPA do the following: 
• 
EPA must ensure the full dataset on human health hazard is reviewed for vinyl chloride, including 
studies used by other government or regulatory bodies to assess vinyl chloride. VI has identified 
additional studies through an independent literature review.  
• 
EPA must appropriately review and characterize occupational exposure studies and data, in light 
of the increased worker protections implemented under the OSHA vinyl chloride standard, the 
PVC MACT rule, the Hazardous Organic NESHAP (HON) rule, and other regulatory developments 
that have dramatically reduced vinyl chloride exposures. This includes a careful assessment of the 
context in which occupational exposure data was generated when evaluating its appropriateness 
for the use in risk evaluation of a specific condition of use. 
• 
EPA must recognize that de minimis concentrations and exposures do not contribute to risk. 
I. Sufficiency of the Hazard Database 
Vinyl chloride is a robustly studied chemical with respect to hazard. Several agency assessments of vinyl 
chloride hazard have already been conducted, including the toxicological profile from the Agency for Toxic 
Substances and Disease Registry,4 the Integrated Risk Information System (IRIS) assessment,5 and an 
assessment by the Texas Commission for Environmental Quality.6 Other examples of assessments of vinyl 
chloride were outlined by EPA in its prioritization document for vinyl chloride. Based on these 
assessments, it is anticipated that vinyl chloride has few relevant7 hazard-based data gaps.  Human health 
hazard data is available to address all major categories of toxicity, including for acute, repeated dose, 
cancer, genotoxicity/mutagenicity, developmental and reproductive toxicity, immunotoxicity, 
 
