neurotoxicity, and toxicokinetics. As noted above, EPA has identified studies in the HAWC on the human 
health hazards of vinyl chloride that contributed to the proposed prioritization of vinyl chloride, which are 
anticipated to inform the pending risk evaluation of vinyl chloride following prioritization. VI reviewed the 
HAWC database to ensure the methods for identification of studies were comprehensive and no 
important hazard studies were missed in EPA’s review.   
As outlined in the prioritization document, EPA utilized the TSCA Draft Systematic Review Protocol8 for 
conducting chemical-specific literature searches. This method aims to conduct a comprehensive search 
for information across a variety of disciplines and sources related to a specific chemical by using a broad 
search phrase specific only to the chemical of interest. EPA conducted its search in January 2023 and 
utilized the following search screen across titles, abstracts, and key words in ProQuest, PubMed, Scopus 
and Web of Science databases9: ""vinyl chloride"" OR ""75-01-4"" OR ""vinyl chloride monomer"" OR 
""chloroethylene"" OR ""chloroethene"" OR ""chloride, vinyl"" OR ""Ultron"" OR ""1-chloroethylene"" OR 
""monochloroethylene"" OR ""monochloroethene"" OR ""chlorethylene"" OR ""1chloroethene"" OR ""ethene, 
chloro-"" OR “Trovidur"" OR “vinyl chlorine” OR ""vinylchloride"". EPA then utilized SWIFT-Review technology 
to filter references and compared its research results to additional search results from endpoint- and 
population-specific searches, and SWIFT-Active Screener to predict which studies met the inclusion 
criteria. EPA screened the identified studies by characteristics related to the population, exposure, 
comparison or setting, and outcome of interest. In its human health hazard assessment, EPA identified 
2,388 references, 760 of which met inclusion criteria at the title and abstract screen. Ultimately, HAWC 
included 169 studies after full text review, including 141 peer-reviewed studies and 28 gray literature 
articles. 
To confirm the results of EPA’s literature search as reported in HAWC, VI pulled all the references from 
the aforementioned ATSDR profile, IRIS assessment, and TCEQ assessment, which produced a list of 780 
deduplicated references. While EPA states in the HAWC database that the Agency considered studies cited 
in the ATSDR and IRIS assessments, it is unclear if those references were systematically reviewed. EPA did 
not consider the TCEQ assessment in its review. VI also performed an independent literature search in 
PubMed on October 7, 2024, utilizing the same chemical search phrase as EPA and adding terms specific 
to human health hazard, including “hazard,"" ""human health hazard,"" ""toxicity,"" ""health,"" ""epidemiology,"" 
and ""toxicology"". The PubMed search identified 1,377 articles. The ATSDR, TCEQ and IRIS and PubMed 
reference lists were compared to the total number of 2,388 articles identified by HAWC to determine if 
EPA’s search, prior to use of any screening criteria, missed any studies.  
 
