VI identified an initial list of 171 articles relevant to human health hazard from ATSDR, TCEQ, IRIS, or 
PubMed that were not captured in the HAWC human health hazard assessment, or any HAWC assessment 
(Appendix I). There are a number of reasons that our search may have different results compared to EPA’s 
assessment. First, EPA completed its literature search nearly 22 months ago, and in that span of time 
approximately 200 new vinyl chloride papers have been published. Specifically, the same search phrase 
that identified 4,120 studies in PubMed in January of 2023 identified 4,320 studies in October of 2024.  
Additionally, EPA reported that it identified 295 studies in the HAWC human health hazard assessment 
that met EPA’s inclusion criteria (“Meets PECO criteria at TIAB Screening”) but were ultimately excluded 
from further review because PDFs of the studies were unavailable. This is generally an acceptable 
exclusion criterion for the purposes of filtering out online databases, book chapters, or other 
miscellaneous sources. However, it appears that EPA inaccurately categorized several peer-reviewed 
journal publications to this exclusion bin that do have PDFs available. In fact, several potentially relevant 
studies in this category have open access PDFs available without any additional cost or access barriers. 
For example – “Reanalysis of updated mortality among vinyl and polyvinyl chloride workers: Confirmation 
of historical evidence and new findings” by Gennaro et al. (2008) is freely available online here10; and 
“Deficiency of N-methylpurine-DNA-glycosylase expression in nonparenchymal cells, the target cell for 
vinyl chloride and vinyl fluoride” by Holt et al. (2000) is freely available online here11. EPA reports that the 
Agency uses a machine-learning tool, SWIFT-Active Screener to increase the efficiency of their title and 
abstract screening. The developers of the tool state that that SWIFT-Active Screener achieves at least 95% 
recall.12  However, this suggests that up to 5% of relevant studies may be missed using this tool. 
Additionally, it is unclear what percentage of total studies the EPA researchers screened. As stated by EPA 
in their Draft Systematic Review Protocol, “EPA screens 95 percent of the references predicted by the 
algorithm to be relevant for the discipline and chemical being screened” (p. 42). Furthermore, the 
Updated Search Strategies document published in 202413 specifically states that “References not 
identified for these disciplines using the SWIFT-Review strategies do not undergo TIAB screening.” (p. 5) 
These SWIFT-Review strategies involve applying discipline-specific search terms established by EPA and 
the developer of the SWIFT-Review tool to the full literature review results as the first pass of the 
screening process. The sources inaccurately binned as “PDF unavailable” may be one example of the 
