Further, it is notable that EPA conducted five different assessments by independent teams including 
general population, consumer, and environmental exposures; physical and chemical properties, 
environmental release and occupational exposure; environmental fate and transport; and environmental 
and human health hazard. While the aim of VI’s review was specific to human health hazard, VI cross 
referenced the list of studies identified in VI’s literature searches to the total list of 7,165 articles identified 
across all five HAWC assessments (including duplicates across assessments) in order to remove studies 
that may not have been captured in the HAWC human health hazard assessment but were covered in the 
other HAWC assessments. VI did not evaluate the appropriateness of EPA classification of studies between 
the five assessments, thus there may be some that are captured within other sections of HAWC that also 
address hazard. For example, Collins et al. (2014)14 conducted a study of angiosarcoma of the liver (ASL) 
surveillance among vinyl chloride workers that was captured in HAWC’s general population, consumer, 
and environmental exposure assessment, but was not considered for the human health hazard 
assessment.   
Based on our independent review, we urge EPA to consider the additional studies identified by VI and 
listed in Appendix I.  Furthermore, given the volume of literature our assessment identified as missing, 
EPA may want to consider conducting supplementary searches for other categories to ensure no critical 
studies are missed on endpoints beyond human health hazard. 
a. Recommendations to EPA on Use of Hazard Data 
Based on the outcomes of this literature search and knowledge on the hazards of vinyl chloride, VI 
recommends EPA consider a few critical points when evaluating studies addressing vinyl chloride hazards, 
particularly epidemiological studies. The available literature spans many decades, and the quality and 
conclusions of studies may vary depending on the data available and the timing of study execution. 
Therefore, we recommend, EPA take caution on using single-study findings regarding cancer endpoints 
that may not be supported by the weight of the literature, and on disregarding qualitative context of 
individual studies when assessing the utility of the study for weight of evidence assessments. 
The primary outcomes proposed to be associated with vinyl chloride exposure in the epidemiological 
literature, including ASL and brain cancer, are exceptionally rare and often result in studies reporting risk 
estimates based on a small number of cases. Additionally, there is significant variation in studies of vinyl 
chloride workers, including study design, sample size, length of follow-up and time frame, with worker 
employment beginning as early as the 1930s and as recently as the 1990s. Heightened attention toward 
vinyl chloride in the 1970s increased the controls around its use and production, resulting in highly 
variable worker exposure levels across time periods. Further, given the small number of cases observed 
 
14 Collins JJ, Jammer B, Sladeczek FM et al. (2014). Surveillance for angiosarcoma of the liver among vinyl chloride 
workers. Journal of Occupational and Environmental Medicine 56(11):1207-1209. 

