in individual studies, researchers may feel compelled to prepare meta-estimates of risk across studies, 
and VI recommends that EPA carefully consider how the variability in study characteristics reduces the 
validity of such estimates. Lack of robust exposure data, often a characteristic of historical occupational 
cohort studies, is also an important consideration (discussed further below) for the interpretation of 
epidemiological studies, particularly in the context of the importance of cumulative exposure estimates 
for determining risk.   
Many of the epidemiological studies on vinyl chloride involve exposures to workers prior to the institution 
of the current permissible exposure limit (PEL) under OSHA.  As noted by Fralish and Downs (2023),15 the 
PEL for vinyl chloride was lowered to 1 ppm by OSHA from a previous limit of 500 ppm in late 1974.  
Therefore, exposures prior to 1975 were likely to be much higher than post-1975 and certainly much 
higher than current conditions, especially given exposure controls that have been instituted at 
manufacturing facilities in response to other regulatory requirements (e.g., under the OSHA Vinyl Chloride 
Standard and under the Clean Air Act, as previously summarized in VI’s comments to the initial proposed 
prioritization of vinyl chloride16). While historical studies may be useful for predicting potential hazards at 
high doses, we caution EPA against deriving dose response relationships based solely on high exposure 
studies that do not accurately represent current exposure scenarios, and that also may not reflect dose 
response at low exposure levels. This may be particularly relevant for cancer, as high exposure 
concentrations are more likely to overwhelm DNA repair mechanisms and other biological protections 
against cancer that may be more effective at lower exposure levels.  
Under OSHA, medical surveillance of workers exposed to vinyl chloride above the action level (0.5 ppm) 
is required; such surveillance includes a general physical examination, “with specific attention to detecting 
enlargement of liver, spleen or kidneys, or dysfunction in these organs, and for abnormalities in skin, 
connective tissues and the pulmonary system,” as well as medical history and serum evaluation for total 
bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, serum glutamic oxalacetic transaminase, serum glutamic pyruvic 
transaminase, and gamma glutamyl transpeptidase. Information from such programs may be useful in 
determining if current OELs are sufficiently protective of key health outcomes associated with historical 
vinyl chloride exposure such as angiosarcoma of the liver. Collins, et al. (2014) found no deaths among 
workers exposed to vinyl chloride from angiosarcoma of the liver after 1974, suggesting OSHA’s action to 
reduce the PEL has been effective in reducing worker risk of angiosarcoma of the liver. Notably, EPA did 
not identify Collins, et al. (2014) amongst the studies flagged in the HAWC database as related to Human 
Health Hazard, though it was identified as a study for review related to “General Population, Consumer, 
and Environmental Exposure.”   
In section 2.7.2.1 of the prioritization designation, EPA states that carcinogenicity designations by previous 
assessments range from “may cause cancer” to “a human carcinogen” and cites assessments such as the 
 
