2012 IARC monograph,17 the 2021 NTP assessment,18 the 2000 IRIS assessment, and others. EPA continues 
on to say that these findings are based on a “variety of epidemiological studies,” specifically studies that 
demonstrated an increased risk of liver, brain and lung cancer, liver angiosarcoma, and hepatocellular 
carcinoma. However, VI cautions EPA against overarching statements regarding the results of these 
assessments that may misrepresent the conclusions. For example, in the IARC monograph, the Working 
Group concludes that they did not find strong evidence of an association between exposure to vinyl 
chloride and cancer of the brain (p. 475). IARC also emphasizes the variability in the epidemiological 
literature for vinyl chloride,19 stating that for many endpoints the findings were “inconsistent between 
studies, no exposure-response relationships were observed in the European multicenter study (Ward et 
al., 2001) and, for several of the sites, the numbers of observed/expected cases were small” (p. 475). Most 
of the epidemiological evidence in studies of vinyl chloride workers in older assessments, such as the 2000 
IRIS assessment, were published in the 1970s, when exposures to vinyl chloride would likely have been 
much higher than current conditions. Data quality has varied greatly over time and therefore, we urge 
EPA to carefully consider the quality of the assessments and the studies upon which these assessments 
are based, along with the temporality of the studies that inform each assessment before drawing 
conclusions regarding hazard based on other agency assessments.  As an example, a more recent study 
by Mundt et al. (2017)20 has included dose reconstructions that are critical in moving from simple 
mortality reports on a population to a study that attempts to stratify the exposure and determine 
mortality ratios applicable to more uniformly exposed populations. Of significance, consistent with basic 
principles of epidemiology, when non-exposed persons are included in an exposure population, the 
Standardized Mortality Ratio (SMR) reflects a combination of two different populations, and does not 
represent the true exposure alone.  
Similar to the cautions regarding extrapolation of dose response data from high-exposure epidemiological 
studies to current exposure scenarios, we also caution EPA that predictions of dose response for cancer 
from animal studies are likely to overpredict cancer in humans. Reitz, et al. (1996)21 demonstrated that, 
even once adjusting for internal dose through the use of a physiologically-based pharmacokinetic (PBPK) 
model, cancer potency factors developed on the basis of animal studies overpredict the cancer incidence 
for angiosarcoma of the liver in humans by an order of magnitude. On this basis, Reitz, et al. (1996) 
 
