carcinogenic effects of vinyl chloride in humans may be related to comparatively higher DNA repair 
enzymes in humans vs. rats (again, reinforcing that repair mechanisms in humans at relevant doses may 
play a key role in determining the dose response for vinyl chloride). Furthermore, this study demonstrated 
the importance of using a PBPK model to account for kinetic differences between humans and animals for 
vinyl chloride, as cancer incidence predictions in humans were two orders of magnitude higher without 
pharmacokinetic adjustments. We urge EPA to take these factors into consideration when reviewing the 
literature for hazard and dose response assessments of vinyl chloride. 
II. Suitability of Publicly Available Occupational Exposure Data 
In an independent review of available exposure literature, VI identified potential limitations in both the 
availability and quality of exposure data relevant to manufacturers of vinyl chloride. As such, we reviewed 
the list of references flagged in the HAWC database as relevant to occupational exposure to understand 
which studies EPA may include in their assessment of exposure in the vinyl chloride industry. A total of 
116 peer-reviewed references and 71 gray literature sources were identified by EPA as potentially relevant 
to occupational exposure to vinyl chloride. VI reviewed the references EPA identified to understand which 
may include occupational exposure information relevant to manufacturing of vinyl chloride and/or 
polyvinyl chloride (PVC).  
Of the peer-reviewed literature identified by EPA, 48 of 116 papers were found to have information 
pertaining to worker exposure to vinyl chloride (i.e., described measurements of worker exposure to vinyl 
chloride). Fifteen of the 48 papers with worker exposure information are potentially relevant to 
manufacturers of vinyl chloride and/or PVC, with 10 of the 15 presenting results of epidemiological 
investigations into specific health outcomes. Many of the epidemiology studies included in this selection 
of peer-reviewed publications provided estimates of worker exposure potential through classification into 
exposure groups. For example, Williams et al. (1976)22 stratified worker exposures across four categories: 
very low exposure (less than 0.05 ppm); low exposure (up to 25 ppm); medium exposure (25 to 200 ppm); 
high exposure (>200 ppm). Other studies estimated a uniform air concentration for the entire facility23 or 
a range of air concentrations by job title24. Those studies incorporating exposure information collected 
prior to 1975 may be limited in application, as exposure control practices changed following the 
promulgation of the new OSHA PEL in 1975. Further, while aligned with the intent of protecting worker 
health, the objective of epidemiological studies is to evaluate a causal relationship between exposure to 
 
