Five of the 15 peer-reviewed studies identified as potentially relevant to manufacturing of vinyl chloride 
and/or PVC presented results of personal breathing zone samples and/or area samples collected on 
workers at vinyl chloride and/or PVC manufacturing plants. Data from personal samples are preferred to 
that from area samples for characterizing worker exposures, as they account for variability in worker 
exposures associated with movement; thus, use of studies reporting only on area sampling should be 
done with caution (and only when necessary) and associated limitations on interpretation recognized.  
Furthermore, context around any collected samples used to characterize worker exposure must be 
acknowledged to understand the representativeness of the data.  While our initial review of the literature 
included in the HAWC database did not include a full study quality assessment, key limitations of the data 
quality in available studies were identified during our initial review. Four25,26,27,28 of the five studies 
evaluated worker exposures to understand the experience of specific health outcomes following exposure 
to vinyl chloride, and one study29 sought to validate a new methodology for biological monitoring of 
workers. We urge EPA to consider the purpose of sampling when evaluating the relevance of a given study 
for characterizing worker exposure to vinyl chloride. Exposure studies with specific objectives may not 
provide a representative estimate of exposure during normal operating conditions. 
 
