conducted at the request of an employer, employee, or union official where there are concerns around 
workplace conditions or in response to health concerns, and therefore are more likely to be representative 
of worst-case conditions or high-end exposure potential than a typical scenario. The Dow (1991) 
occupational health summary report presents the results of vinyl chloride sampling conducted on the vinyl 
chloride monomer production unit at a Dow Chemical plant in Freeport, Texas. However, information on 
the data collection methods, the raw sampling results, process descriptions, and other qualitative 
information critical to interpretation of the results is not included in the report. While the gray literature 
resources identified by EPA do provide useful information to quantitatively estimate exposure potential 
among vinyl chloride and/or PVC manufacturing workers, the age of the studies and limited qualitative 
information provided call into question the reliability of the data for representing current conditions of 
use.  
Based on this review, we recommend EPA carefully consider the available worker exposure literature. The 
context of the studies upon which the Agency relies for risk evaluation and/or the available data within 
those studies must be appropriate for characterizing current worker exposures to vinyl chloride.  We 
advise EPA to avoid reliance on studies that include exposures prior to 1975, contain data that represents 
emergency or incident-based exposure estimates, lack personal air sampling data, omit necessary 
qualitative information regarding the purpose of sampling and/or activities that occurred during sampling, 
and/or was conducted for purposes outside of characterizing baseline exposures to vinyl chloride.   
