The vinyl industry in the United States is strongly committed to the safety of its products and the well-
being of our employees and the communities in which we operate. Many regulatory agencies have long 
recognized that de minimis concentrations and exposures do not contribute to risk. These include a variety 
of EPA programs, as well as programs administered by the Occupational Safety and Health Administration 
(OSHA), and are described in more detail in the sections below. EPA must give weight to this experience 
as it reviews vinyl chloride.   
Under TSCA, EPA has incorporated a de minimis exemption into several proposed and final section 6 risk 
management rules after finding that these concentrations and exposures do not present an unreasonable 
risk. However, as a matter of policy, EPA should not wait until a potential risk management stage to make 
it clear that such scenarios do not contribute to risk and do not require regulation. EPA can improve its 
approach by either excluding de minimis concentrations and exposures from the scope of the risk 
evaluation (and, thus, risk management) or implementing a screening approach that, for example, makes 
it more explicit in the draft risk evaluation (or earlier) that de minimis concentrations and exposures do 
not present unreasonable risk.   
As explained in previous comments to EPA in this proceeding,35 manufacturing, processing, and use of 
vinyl chloride is highly regulated and highly controlled. PVC manufacturing facilities are required by Clean 
Air Act regulations to use control technology (steam strippers) to remove residual vinyl chloride from PVC 
resin.36 When compared to standard de minimis thresholds, the stripped resin limits in the PVC MACT are 
comparatively very low. In addition, it is important to note that these limits apply to resin slurry. 
Subsequent processing steps, including processing steps at the PVC manufacturing site and at 
downstream processing sites (e.g., compounding and extrusion) may further reduce the concentration of 
residual vinyl chloride in the final product.  Accordingly, to the extent that finished PVC products contain 
vinyl chloride at all, it is only present as an impurity at very low levels, which minimizes exposure.  
a. De Minimis Exemptions Under TSCA Section 6  
One recently finalized risk management rule and two proposed risk management rules include de minimis 
exemptions of 0.1 percent.37 In the final methylene chloride risk management rule,38 EPA included an 
exemption from all prohibitions and restrictions for products that contain methylene chloride at a 
concentration of less than 0.1% by weight.39  EPA used the 0.1% threshold on the basis that methylene 
 
