degreasing products would achieve exposure concentrations at or below the ECEL based on a near-field 
8-hour TWA of 0.145 ppm at the 95th percentile.”44 Thus, the 0.1% de minimis exemption for PCE is 
conservative (on top of EPA’s already conservative approach to section 6 rules).   
In the proposed n-methylpyrrolidone (NMP) risk management rule, EPA included an exemption from all 
prohibitions and restrictions for products that contain NMP at a concentration of less than 0.1% by 
weight.45 Although NMP was not determined by EPA to be a carcinogen, EPA used a 0.1% threshold 
because of NMP’s classification in the EU as a substance of very high concern.46 EPA stated that the Agency 
conducted an analysis of the weight fraction of NMP in products for the most conservative use, finding 
that “an NMP concentration of 0.1% would achieve exposure concentrations that do not contribute to 
unreasonable risk up to an air concentration of 30 mg/m3.”47 
