i. Toxics Release Inventory De Minimis Threshold 
The Toxics Release Inventory (TRI) program under the Superfund Amendments includes the familiar de 
minimis exemptions of 0.1% and 1.0% from threshold and release reporting calculations.50 These 
exemptions apply to carcinogens and all other substances, respectively. In adopting these thresholds, EPA 
sought consistency with OSHA’s regulations.51 Although the de minimis exemption no longer applies to 
chemicals of special concern,52 vinyl chloride is not a chemical of special concern under the TRI program.53 
ii. OSHA Hazard Communication Standard De Minimis Thresholds 
OSHA’s Hazard Communication Standard (HCS) provides threshold concentrations for mixtures. As 
discussed above, many other de minimis regulatory exemptions follow the approach established in the 
HCS. EPA has relied on the HCS approach in implementing de minimis exemptions in risk management 
rules, stating that “As a matter of risk management policy, EPA believes that the widespread awareness 
by industrial and commercial workplaces of the de minimis levels in the OSHA Hazard Communication 
Standard would generally support successful implementation of the level EPA has identified.”54   
Under the HCS, OSHA provides a variety of de minimis thresholds, which distinguish between carcinogens, 
mutagens, and all other substances. These de minimis thresholds include: 
