the early 1980s. In the 1982 proposed rule, OSHA set these thresholds “recognizing that there is no 
scientifically correct delineation, but that the one percent cut-off is apparently considered reasonable by 
a number of parties.”58 Nevertheless, these thresholds clearly have been found to be suitably protective, 
as they have remained unchanged and been incorporated into many other regulatory regimes worldwide. 
Indeed, at the time of promulgation OSHA noted that use of a 1% threshold rather than a higher number 
may be overprotective.59  
