Subject: Protect frontline communities by banning vinyl chloride -- Proposed High-Priority 
Substance Designations under the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA); Notice of Availability 
(Document ID: EPA-HQ-OPPT-2018-0448-0591) 
 
To EPA Administrator Michael Regan and Office of Chemical Safety and Pollution Prevention 
Assistant Administrator Michal Freedhoff: 
 
I strongly urge you to take strong action to protect frontline communities from the harmful effects 
of vinyl chloride. As a concerned citizen, I believe it is crucial to prioritize the health and safety 
of vulnerable populations exposed to this dangerous chemical. 
 
It’s been almost 50 years since vinyl chloride was identified as a human carcinogen and it is 
long overdue for a comprehensive risk evaluation that should lead to the Environmental 
Protection Agency banning this dangerous chemical. Vinyl chloride is known to be harmful to 
public health and the environment, and with safer alternatives available on the market, the 
continued use of vinyl chloride is not only not necessary, but dangerous. I applaud the 
Environmental Protection Agency for beginning that important process and urge you to 
designate vinyl chloride as a high-priority chemical under the Toxic Substances Control Act 
(TSCA) and to move it through the TSCA process as quickly as possible with the ultimate goal 
of completely banning this toxic chemical. 
 
Over 10 billion pounds of vinyl chloride are produced on average annually in the U.S., primarily 
to make polyvinyl chloride (PVC) plastic. Historical usage and disposal of vinyl chloride have led 
to widespread contamination, particularly in Superfund sites nationwide, where the chemical 
continues to leach l into groundwater. Communities near manufacturing facilities, disposal sites, 
and transportation routes are at the highest risk of exposure. These often include low-income 
communities, communities of color, and other marginalized populations already burdened with 
greater environmental health risks. The devastating consequences of vinyl chloride exposure 
are evident in places like Louisiana, where entire communities have been forced to relocate due 
to PVC facility pollution. 
 
Air releases of vinyl chloride have averaged around 500,000 pounds per year since 2010, 
increasing cancer risks for frontline communities near vinyl chloride manufacturing facilities. 
Inhaling vinyl chloride can lead to short term health harms such as acute breathing problems 
like coughing, nose bleeds, and bronchitis, while chronic exposure may lead to asthma and 
other pulmonary disorders. It also affects the central nervous system, causing dizziness, 
headaches, fatigue, and visual disturbances. Severe cases can lead to neuropathy with 
symptoms like numbness, tingling, and pain in the extremities. Those with ongoing exposure—
such as fenceline community members and vinyl and PVC manufacturing and processing 
workers – are at increased risk of health harms including cancer, nerve damage, liver damage, 
and lowered immunity. 
 

Page 2 of 2 
 
Workers in vinyl chloride and PVC manufacturing and disposal industries face significant 
occupational exposure, which can result in severe health issues such as cancers, respiratory 
problems, and neurological effects. From 2010 to 2023, nearly 1,000 incidents involving vinyl 
chloride, including leaks, spills, fires, and explosions, have been reported—an average of one 
every 5.3 days. These incidents highlight the chemical’s high reactivity and potential for 
catastrophic events, especially in densely populated areas and along rail routes used for 
transport. 
 
In a January 2024 report, Toxic Cargo, Toxic-Free Future showed that, at any given time, 36 
million pounds of vinyl chloride are on more than 200 rail cars across nearly 2,000 miles of U.S. 
railways. These chemicals supply PVC plastics factories in New Jersey, Illinois, and Niagara 
Falls, Ontario. These rail shipments of vinyl chloride to make PVC plastic put more than three 
million people at risk, in communities from Texas to New Jersey. The frontline communities near 
vinyl chloride manufacturing are particularly vulnerable to its adverse effects. The 2023 train 
derailment, spill, and combustion of vinyl chloride in East Palestine, Ohio was just the latest 
example of how dangerous this chemical can be, and why it needs to be banned from use in 
plastic production nationwide. 
 
Old landfills and toxic waste sites—often sited in low-income and minority communities—leak 
vinyl chloride into groundwater. PVC drinking water pipes may leach residual vinyl chloride into 
the water supply and threaten public health, and consumer products including children’s toys 
can also leach residual vinyl chloride. 
 
This is a clear environmental justice issue that demands immediate attention and action. I 
strongly urge the Environmental Protection Agency to: 
• 
Implement stricter regulations on the production, use, and disposal of vinyl chloride. 
• 
Increase monitoring and enforcement in areas with PVC facilities. 
• 
Provide resources for community relocation and health support in affected areas. 
• 
Invest in research for safer alternatives to vinyl chloride in industrial processes. 
• 
Engage directly with affected communities to address their specific concerns and needs. 
 
Continuing to use vinyl chloride in plastic products will put humans and wildlife at unnecessary 
risk. The EPA has a responsibility to protect all citizens, especially those most vulnerable to 
environmental hazards. Given the extensive and well-documented harms of vinyl chloride and 
the high likelihood of continued accidents and harmful exposures, the Environmental Protection 
Agency must protect our communities and environment by banning vinyl chloride as soon as 
possible. By taking decisive action on vinyl chloride, you can make a significant impact on public 
health and environmental justice. 
 
“The ultimate test of a moral society is the kind of world that it leaves to its children.” 
-- Dietrich Bonhoeffer 
 
Thank you for your consideration of my comments. Please do NOT add my name to your 
mailing list. I will learn about future developments on this issue from other sources. 
 
Sincerely, 
Christopher Lish 
San Rafael, CA"
