Dear Ms. Au: 
ASC appreciates the opportunity to comment on the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency’s 
(EPA’s or Agency’s) notice proposing to designate five chemicals as high priority under Section 
6(b) of TSCA.1  
The Adhesive and Sealant Council (ASC or Council) is a trade association representing the 
North American adhesive and sealant value chain. The Council is comprised of 117 adhesive and 
sealant manufacturers, raw material and equipment suppliers and distributors, and industry 
consultants, representing more than 75 percent of the U.S. industry. Offering education, 
legislative advocacy, professional networking, and business growth solutions to its members, the 
ASC is the center of knowledge and a catalyst for industry growth on a global basis for 
manufacturers, suppliers, and end-users.  
EPA is proposing to designate acetaldehyde (CASRN 75-07-0), acrylonitrile (CASRN 107-13-
1), benzenamine (CASRN 62-53-3), vinyl chloride (CASRN 75-01-4), and 4,4-methylene bis(2-
chloroaniline) (MBOCA) (CASRN 101-14-4) as high-priority substances for future risk 
evaluations. A high-priority substance is one that EPA determines, without consideration of costs 
or other non-risk factors, may present an unreasonable risk of injury to health or the environment 
because of a potential hazard and a potential route of exposure under the conditions of use. EPA 
considers factors such as hazard and exposure potential, potentially exposed subpopulations, 
persistence and bioaccumulation, production volume, and storage of the chemical near 
significant sources of drinking water. 
 
 
1 89 Fed. Reg. 60420 (July 25, 2024).   

 
2 
A number of the chemicals being considered for prioritization are used in the adhesive and 
sealant industry, as identified by EPA’s high priority designation documents. Our membership 
offers the following input at this stage for two of these chemicals: acetaldehyde and acrylonitrile.  
 
Acetaldehyde  
 
We have members where acetaldehyde is not a raw material itself, but it is present as a residual 
substance in certain raw materials used to manufacture adhesives and sealants. Acetaldehyde is 
present as a residual in raw materials in extremely low amounts, ranging from only <=0.0009% 
to 0.05%. In the finished products in which these raw materials are used, acetaldehyde ranges in 
concentration from 0.00028% to 0.0053%. Therefore, exposure levels both at the manufacturing 
level and at the consumer levels are very minimal.  
 
Acrylonitrile 
 
We have members where acrylonitrile is only present as an impurity in raw materials used at 
0.0025%. Therefore, like acetaldehyde, exposure levels are extremely low.  
 
 
Based on the extremely low presence of these chemicals in raw materials, our industry would 
support a de minimis exemption for both acetaldehyde and acrylonitrile should EPA pursue 
further risk evaluation or regulation of these substances for uses in the adhesive and sealant 
industry.  
 
