I. 
Introduction 
The American Chemistry Council (ACC)1 is pleased to submit these comments regarding 
the TSCA prioritization process.  Below please find our remarks organized as general comments, 
including the information in the related report for each chemical representing the prioritization 
screening review mandated under 40 CFR 702.9(a); comments supporting the evaluation of 
individual chemical candidates; and comments on the document accompanying the high priority 
designations – Updated Search Strategies Used to Identify Potentially Relevant Discipline-
Specific Information.  
II. 
General Comments 
The designation of chemicals as high-priority substances for risk evaluations was 
mandated by TSCA [15 U.S.C. § 2605(b)(1)] and the process is codified in EPA regulations (40 
CFR Part 702, Subpart A).  The 9- to 12-month period of the prioritization process and the 
release of the screening review as part of the proposed high priority designation represents an 
opportunity for EPA to collect available information and signal to stakeholders, particularly 
manufacturers and users, where emphasis might be placed in the risk evaluation and additional 
data that would inform those analyses.   
ACC commends EPA on the modifications to the process and is pleased that “EPA now 
has a head start on risk evaluations.”2  ACC has additional recommendations on how EPA could 
further use the prioritization stage to enhance the impending risk evaluations.   
A. 
The Prioritization Screening Review Should Include Clear Benchmarks. 
The statute requires EPA’s process to designate the priority of chemical substances to 
include a consideration of: 
‒ The hazard and exposure potential of a chemical substance, including 
consideration of persistence and bioaccumulation; potentially exposed or 
 
