Harmful Chemicals” 

High-Priority Designations Under TSCA 
 
EPA-HQ-OPPT-2023-0601 
October 23, 2024 
 
Page 3 
 
 
susceptible subpopulations, and storage near significant sources of drinking 
water; 
‒ The conditions of use or significant changes in the conditions of use of the 
chemical substance; and 
‒ The volume or significant changes in the volume of the chemical substance 
manufactured or processed. 
The agency restates these statutory considerations in its Framework Rule for 
Prioritization without change, except that the agency added an additional catch-all provision: 
“other risk-based criteria that EPA determines to be relevant to the designation of the chemical 
substance’s priority.” 40 C.F.R. § 702.9(a)(7).   
The prioritization screening review also should include clear benchmarks related to the 
considerations that signal whether the information associated with the subject chemical indicates 
that it would be a low priority or a high priority for risk evaluation.  For example, what are the 
benchmarks associated with the hazard profile, exposure potential, persistence, bioaccumulation, 
etc. that would land a chemical on one side or the other of the low-high priority divide, and how 
are those individual results for each criterion incorporated into a final decision on the chemical’s 
priority?  To be clear, we are not advocating for numerical criteria, per se, but more context 
around the identified information for each element and how that affects EPA’s decision-making. 
B. 
The Prioritization Screening Review Should Inform Future Risk Evaluation of the 
Subject Chemical. 
EPA should take advantage of the prioritization process to inform the years-long risk 
evaluation process.  EPA’s risk evaluation process could be improved by including a 
comprehensive decision framework that satisfies the needs of the problem formulation for the 
tiered human health and ecological risk assessments.  Such a decision framework would clearly 
identify the questions that are being answered, and the outcome at each decision point based on 
the answer (e.g., a trivial risk that requires no further evaluation, a risk of concern at a screening 
level that warrants further refinement of the assessment).  Moreover, the risk evaluation should 
clearly inform risk management.  EPA’s figure showing the risk evaluation process, which 
shows the clear relationship of Prioritization and Risk Evaluation to Risk Management (Figure 
1), envisions that the Agency will develop a risk evaluation that informs the risk management 
phase of the process.    

