While it would be premature to propose risk management steps before the risk evaluation 
is finalized, understanding potential risk management measures and their effectiveness can help 
those responsible for developing the risk evaluation provide information to facilitate an effective 
risk management regulation.  For example, if implementation of a new occupational exposure 
limit is a plausible outcome for control of exposures associated with unreasonable occupational 
risks, the risk evaluation should fully explore existing occupational exposure limits and current 
standard practice and best practice for exposure control to achieve such a limit.   
C. 
EPA Should Distinguish Between Conditions of Use (COUs) Where the Chemical 
Has a Functional Purpose in the Use and Those COUs Where It May Be Present 
at Trace Levels as a Byproduct or Impurity With No Function. 
The prioritization screening review (i.e., the proposed designation as a high-priority 
substance for risk evaluation document) contains a table of conditions of use based on chemical 
data reporting (CDR) data from recent reporting cycles.  It would be valuable for EPA to 
distinguish between COUs where the chemical has a functional purpose and those COUs where 
the chemical may be present as a byproduct or impurity.  For example, the benzenamine 
screening review identifies commercial and consumer use of benzenamine in rubber tires based 
on 2016 CDR reporting, and antioxidants/antiozonants in rubber tires for consumer use based on 
2020 CDR reporting.  However, based on comments from the U.S. Tire Manufacturers 
Association (USTMA), a trade association of manufacturers of tires in the United States: 
Benzenamine Is Used by Chemical Manufacturers as a Starting Material to 
Synthesize Accelerators and Antioxidants Which are Added to Tires to Optimize 

