B. 
EPA Should Use Available Data and Scientific Methods to Make Appropriate 
Screening Decisions Early in the Risk Evaluation Process for Existing Chemicals.  
EPA has consistently and publicly relayed its concerns regarding time and resources for 
TSCA actions. Given these concerns, as well as TSCA’s mandates to reduce animal testing and 
ever-present responsibilities to prevent government waste, EPA’s reluctance to adopt established 
practices within the regulatory scientific community (such as the threshold of toxicological 
concern or TTC) that streamline the risk evaluation process while still being public health 
protective is perplexing.  
For TSCA prioritization, a peer reviewed study entitled “Utilizing Threshold of 
Toxicological Concern (TTC) with High Throughput Exposure Predictions (HTE) as a Risk-
Based Prioritization Approach for Thousands of Chemicals” was published in 2018.8  This study, 
for which an EPA ORD scientist was lead (and corresponding) author, was a proof-of-concept 
investigation that described how TTC and HTE could be combined in a risk-based approach and 
used as a first step for screening thousands of chemicals on the TSCA inventory.   
The TTC/HTE approach is not limited to prioritization and could also inform risk 
evaluations. Given the need to consider multiple routes of exposure and aggregate exposures in 
TSCA risk evaluation (as well as the fundamental toxicological consideration that it is ultimately 
internal exposure, and not external exposure, that mediates systemic toxicity beyond portal-of-
entry effects), ACC (through its Long-Range Research Initiative (LRI) has funded research to 
facilitate development of an internal TTC (or iTTC) approach.9,10   
A number of authoritative bodies regularly apply the TTC approach to address chemicals 
with unknown toxicity, including the U.S. Food and Drug Administration and the European Food 
Safety Authority (EFSA).11  To prevent government waste, and to make the most efficient use of 
 
