the limited resources on the TSCA program, EPA should apply the TTC approach as well.  In 
this regard, as part of ACC’s LRI NAM research program, a study entitled “Value of information 
case study strongly supports the use of Threshold of Toxicological Concern (TTC)”12 was 
published as an open access article in the peer reviewed literature earlier this year.  This study 
established that  using TTC to derive a toxicity reference value (TRV) for substances with 
limited data  “was shown to provide equivalent or greater health protection, immense return on 
investment (ROI), greater net benefit, and substantially lower costs of delay (CoD) compared 
with TRVs derived from either traditional human health assessment (THHA) chronic toxicity 
testing in lab animals or the 5-day in vivo EPA Transcriptomic Assessment Product (ETAP).” 
Given the scientific basis provided above, as well the value of information analysis, EPA 
should thoroughly consider and document processes such as TTC with HTE and iTTC as part of 
the prioritization screening review.  The results of the application of such approaches would 
support credible screening decisions early in the risk evaluation process for existing chemicals. 
IV. 
Comments Regarding EPA’s Updated Search Strategies Used to Identify Potentially 
Relevant Discipline-Specific Information 
ACC appreciates that EPA is publicly sharing its search strategy process to support 
decision-making for TSCA Section 6 risk evaluations.  However, the process and Updated 
Search Strategies document is narrowly focused on a limited aspect of the risk evaluation 
process, the initial stages of the process.  It is unclear what comes next and how information 
identified through the process will be used to make final decisions.   
A. 
EPA Should Explain the Role of the Updated Search Strategies within the Larger 
Risk Evaluation Process. 
i. 
EPA should develop and maintain a definitive list of references that have been 
captured and included in the universe of information applied across the entire 
TSCA Section 6 risk evaluation process. 
Each of the substance-specific high-priority designation documents describes the 
identification of literature and its screening.  The documents then direct the reader to the Health 
Assessment Workplace Collaborative (HAWC) website for the results of the substance-specific 
and element-specific literature search, screening and review intended to support the designation.  
For the purposes of low/high priority designation, those HAWC are likely sufficient, however, 
 
