ACC understands that there are significant gaps in the current chemical-specific literature 
databases for the purposes of risk evaluation.  While it is understandable that EPA will continue 
to collect literature through stakeholder engagement such as this comment period for the 
proposed priority designations, EPA should be transparent regarding the evolution of that 
literature database.  EPA needs a transparent, curated and reliable database so all stakeholders 
can understand whether EPA has conducted a thorough search of available data, and whether 
literature that has been submitted by stakeholders has been incorporated into that database. 
The proposed designation documents have vague statements regarding the purpose of the 
HAWC database and the future status of its use.  For example, in the Proposed Designation of 
Vinyl Chloride as a High-Priority Substance for Risk Evaluation it states: 
‒ …as additional relevant information is identified, the interactive versions of these 
figures may change. (p. 73) 
‒ Should this chemical be designated as a High-Priority Substance, screening 
decisions and data elements characterized in literature inventory trees and 
evidence maps presented in Appendices C.1, C.2, C.3, C.4, C.5 for discipline-
specific interactive visualizations may be updated for future actions. (p. 73) 
‒ Additional data may be added to the interactive version as they become 
available. (Figure_Apx C-1 through Figure_Apx C-10) 
Having a HAWC literature database for each chemical undergoing TSCA Section 6 
prioritization and potentially risk evaluation could be a valuable resource for all stakeholders to 
understand the available literature for the substance.  However, EPA should not treat such a 
database casually.  Stakeholders need to know that EPA will commit the resources necessary to 
support such a tool and keep it current.  Maintaining this information in a publicly accessible 
electronic database will foster trust with the agency and among various stakeholders.  
ii. 
EPA should clarify how it will assess screened documents during the review of 
the full text/full report.  
 
The description of the Search Strategies process appears to stop at the title and abstract 
search and screening.  EPA should further describe subsequent steps in the process including 
how information for sources screened in will be reviewed during the full text/full report stage.  
This description should include the role of data quality assessment in later stages and whether 
information would be synthesized in any capacity during those later stages. 
iii. 
EPA should clarify whether and how the search strategy would be applied 
once a substance is designated as a high-priority chemical. 

