It is unclear how the search strategy will be integrated with the systematic review process 
once a substance is designated as a high-priority chemical.  EPA should further describe whether 
a new search would be conducted or the existing search amended.  Moreover, EPA’s 
expectations of stakeholders’ responsibilities in providing data and information should be 
articulated.  Likewise, EPA’s conceptualization of the relationship between the search strategy 
and the risk evaluation scoping process should be explained.   
B. 
EPA Should Better Describe Limitations Associated with the SWIFT Platform. 
The search strategy relies heavily on the “Sciome Workbench for Interactive Computer-
Facilitated Text-mining” (SWIFT) platform (i.e., SWIFT-Review and SWIFT-ActiveScreener 
tools), which presents a number of potential limitations and concerns. 
i. 
ACC has identified a number of limitations of the SWIFT platform that 
obscure the understanding of the relevancy and reliability of results. 
‒ It is unclear why each discipline is treated differently within the SWIFT 
literature prioritization process. 
‒ It is unclear what manual validation steps have been performed, including 
testing of classification scores, cut-off thresholds, and the reliability of 
those processes due to a lack of reporting. 
‒ SWIFT is a proprietary platform developed by Sciome that has multiple 
products. The process as described starts with SWIFT-Review, which 
includes classification scores and a cut-off or threshold for all literature 
falling below such. Eligible literature is then transferred to SWIFT-
ActiveScreener which applies additional prioritization and determines 
when all relevant literature has been identified. The process appears 
duplicative and it is unclear what value SWIFT-Review provides, as it 
may also introduce bias or increase risk for missing critical studies. In 
addition, a number of studies are cut out with these processes and it is 
unclear whether there are steps to ensure that important information is not 
being eliminated.   
ii. 
The search strategy is logical and structured but would be difficult (or 
impossible) to test and reproduce.  
‒ It is difficult to perform a comprehensive validation with the information 
provided in the Updated Search Strategies document.  

