C. 
Specific Comments on the Updated Search Strategies Document 
i. 
Please clarify “these data elements” (p. 3) 
The Updated Search Strategies document states on p. 3, “EPA expects to use information 
received from public comments on these data elements to inform and refine a draft scope of the 
risk evaluation if any of these…”  It is not clear what “these data elements” are.  The results of 
the search? 
ii. 
The Updated Search Strategies document is not comprehensive in that it relies 
on a number of other references and guidance documents.  
The Updated Search Strategies document relies on extensive citing of other documents. 
such as the 2021 Draft Protocol or chemical specific Proposed Designation documents, for 
additional information and, therefore, requires document switching to fully interpret the methods 
used. An increased degree of reporting in a single document would increase clarity on the precise 
methods implemented. Additional issues with citations include: 
‒ The chemical-specific proposed high-priority designation documents (USEPA 2024a-
e) are somewhat difficult to find. Links send straight to HERO; however, there is no 
link in the HERO webpage to the document.  
‒ There is some degree of overlap in the information provided in each of the chemical-
specific proposed high-priority designation documents but it is not clear what is 
similar or different in approaches across all. 
iii. 
ACC notes some inconsistencies in methods between components/disciplines.  
Methods for identification of prioritized literature and subsequent title/abstract screening 
are different for each of the disciplines, however, it is not clear why a common approach was not 
applied to all disciplines. For example, Sections 4 (Phys/Chem) and 5 (Env fate and transport) of 
the Updated Search Strategies outline a discipline-specific syntax developed by EPA ORD and 
OPPT with Sciome used in SWIFT-Review to identify potential references of interest. A similar 
procedure is used for the environmental and human health-related references. All documents that 
were returned by these syntaxes appear to be advanced to Title/Abstract screening (presumably, 
but not clearly stated, in SWIFT-ActiveScreener). Comparatively, Section 6 (occupational 

